## Statement of changes (Apache License 2.0, Section 4(b))

The Codex runtime driver was replaced with the Claude Agent SDK. The scanning
pipeline it drives is reused from Codex Security because it is model-agnostic.

Reused from Codex Security. File counts below were verified by direct comparison
against sdk/typescript/_bundled_plugin in the upstream repository at commit
5625adc159bbbfb4bd85557d83e2805d27379230 (2026-07-31), so this statement can be
re-checked independently:

  - plugin/scripts/  — 31 Python files, 16,672 lines: the scan contract
    finalizer, sealer, validators, and the SQLite workbench. 26 files are
    byte-for-byte identical once the product rename is applied; 5 carry further
    changes, limited to SARIF display branding, the state-directory variable
    name, and two error strings.
  - plugin/schemas/  — the 3 canonical JSON Schemas. All 3 are changed, only to
    rebrand identifier prefixes. The schema `$id` URLs still point at the
    upstream project, which is where the contract is defined.
  - plugin/skills/   — 26 files (13 SKILL.md scan procedures and their
    references); 7 identical, 19 changed. Changes are rebranding, plus the
    deep-scan discovery phase reimplementation described below.
  - plugin/references/ — 8 shared reference documents; 1 identical, 7 rebranded.

New in Claude Security:

  - src/               — 3,500 lines of TypeScript: the CLI, target resolution,
    the Claude Agent SDK driver, usage/cost accounting, scan history, policy
    gating, bulk scanning, the pre-commit hook generator, and credentials.
  - plugin/scripts/workbench_bridge.py — 326 lines; a JSON dispatch layer over
    the workbench functions. Upstream's workbench_cli.py parses arguments only;
    its command implementations are library functions, so a caller outside the
    Codex CLI needs its own entry point.

Reimplemented rather than ported:

  - The repeated-discovery phase of deep-security-scan. Upstream ships that
    orchestration as a compressed build artifact with no readable source, so it
    could not be ported by reading. The phase boundaries, merge discipline, and
    centralized validation tail are preserved; the round scheduling is written
    natively using ordinary subagents. Treat it as a reimplementation of that
    phase, not a byte-for-byte port.


